are companies filled with people who

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:09.760
think probably the models are conscious

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.720
or will be conscious soon.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:13.760
>> You seem to be sympathetic to the idea

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:14.360
that AI could be conscious to some

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:17.400
degree.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:18.760
>> No, no, no, don't confuse um uh be

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:19.960
having an open mind with being

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:20.320
sympathetic.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:22.640
>> Okay.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:25.320
>> No, I'm with you completely

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:26.800
um the AIs just aren't conscious. They

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:28.520
don't have any of that biological

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.240
machinery. Functionalism for

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:30.785
consciousness is hopeless.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:30.880
>> Mhm.

00:00:30.785 --> 00:00:32.759
>> [clears throat]

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:34.080
>> You know, you just all you can have all

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:36.080
the functions you want, you're just not

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:37.680
going to get any feeling out of it. But

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:39.960
why it's the experience of motion

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:41.520
instead of some other experience or no

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:44.080
experience at all, we haven't got a

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:44.080
clue.

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:52.960
>> Hello everybody and welcome to this uh

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:56.600
mini philosophy live conversation today

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:58.880
with Ned Block who is a legend in the

00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:01.040
philosophy space, but especially in the

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:03.920
philosophy of mind.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:06.160
He is the Silver Professor of Philosophy

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:08.000
at New York University.

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:10.880
Silver Professor? What what do you have

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.760
to do to to upgrade to a to gold? Is

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:14.960
that commonly asked?

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:17.290
>> I have heard that joke so many times.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:17.840
>> Sorry, it's always going to be boring.

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:20.600
>> [laughter]

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:21.800
>> No, it's named after Mr. Silver who gave

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:23.840
a bunch of money.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:23.960
>> Okay, fine. Yeah, yeah.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:25.520
>> [laughter]

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:28.560
>> That's what you did. And he recently

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:31.680
joined some fellow thinkers at the ICCS

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:34.320
workshop in the Galapagos to explore

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:35.960
whether AI could be conscious and

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:38.880
whether consciousness depends on

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:41.280
computation alone or requires particular

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:44.720
physical processes. So, welcome Ned.

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:44.720
Thanks for joining us today.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:49.800
Sorry to be boringly analytical, but I

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:53.720
feel as though we have to define our

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:55.720
terms about consciousness because

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:57.480
as far as I

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:59.720
am aware or I feel you know, we all we

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:02.120
have a mind. We all have consciousness

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:05.120
and it seems to me as if the battle

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:06.800
lines have been drawn

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:09.119
by the likes of Chalmers, by the likes

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:12.400
of Nagel with these words qualia, which

00:02:09.119 --> 00:02:15.040
is first-personhood subjectivity. Really

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:16.800
first-personhood is is the definitive

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:19.160
element of consciousness. Like what am I

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:21.480
thinking of right now? That kind of that

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:23.560
workspace in my head and the feelings

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:24.840
that belong to me. Is that how you

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:26.360
understand consciousness or do you

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:27.280
operate from a different understanding

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:29.720
of consciousness?

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:32.480
>> Uh I don't particularly like that way of

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:38.040
of thinking about it because I think

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:39.800
that even a creature who a our system in

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:41.080
our computational

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:43.600
image

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:45.360
but who lacks um

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:47.680
uh phenomenal feels, what I call

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:49.640
phenomenal consciousness.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:51.920
Even such a creature could have a

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:54.000
first-person point of view.

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:57.360
So this comes to the fore in the

00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:00.760
discussion of the current AI models that

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:03.840
we have. I think they have no phenomenal

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:05.920
consciousness, but I think they do have

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:08.600
something that approximates a

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:10.959
first-person point of view.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:12.959
So I don't think the the this talk of

00:03:10.959 --> 00:03:16.519
first and third

00:03:12.959 --> 00:03:18.480
uh person is really that helpful.

00:03:16.519 --> 00:03:20.800
I I the way I like to think of it is I

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:22.760
make a distinction between

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:24.880
phenomenal consciousness, what it feels

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:28.239
like to have an experience

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:30.600
and access consciousness,

00:03:28.239 --> 00:03:33.920
which is the informational content of

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:36.800
that experience being uh broadcast to

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:40.040
many cognitive mechanisms

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:42.800
um all at once. And I think that the the

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:44.480
AIs we have have that access

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:46.920
consciousness or at least they're they

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:48.880
have some signs of it. Uh and I think

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:52.120
you know, there's been some recent uh

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:55.440
papers uh by some uh people at at

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:56.320
Anthropic, one of the big AI companies.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:58.560
Um

00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:00.480
pioneered by a a guy there named Jack

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:04.360
Lindsey. But anyway, I think that they

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:08.040
have some evidence that Claude has some

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:10.480
elements of access consciousness.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:11.760
Uh in particular, elements of a global

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:13.280
workspace. So,

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:15.240
So, I don't like this first-person,

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:17.079
third-person way of thinking about it. I

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:20.239
I I think you're better off with the

00:04:17.079 --> 00:04:22.240
phenomenal consciousness versus access

00:04:20.239 --> 00:04:22.680
consciousness. It's more revealing, I

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:24.320
think.

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:26.640
>> Can we do a a quick tour of the

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:28.040
philosophy of mind? It seems to me as if

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:29.120
there are three different schools of the

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:32.560
philosophy of mind, which is there are

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:34.919
physicalists who say that everything can

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:38.160
be explained or at least understood

00:04:34.919 --> 00:04:39.240
potentially by physical explanations of

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:40.480
phenomena.

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:41.880
There are non-physicalists, which is

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:43.919
also a very broad category, and there's

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:45.240
somewhere in between on a a spectrum

00:04:43.919 --> 00:04:47.200
which we might want to call

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:48.840
emergentists. And they And they kind of

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:50.440
exist in between

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:51.400
these two. So, I was wondering if if we

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:53.320
Let's could dive into some

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:55.840
non-physicalist theories first, and and

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:57.520
you tell me what you think is wrong with

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:58.240
these theories. So, of course, the first

00:04:57.520 --> 00:04:59.640
>> Well, wait a minute. How do you know I

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:01.240
think something's wrong with them?

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:01.560
>> Well, I'm sure you will cuz some of them

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:02.880
are incompatible.

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:03.880
>> I know, but I do because I'm a

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:05.440
physicalist.

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:07.720
>> So, the first one and and the person to

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:09.240
beat in the space is Descartes and the

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:10.720
and dualism.

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:12.840
And the idea that the mind and the body

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:14.320
are two separate separate substances

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:16.000
entirely,

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:17.720
and they interact through something that

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:18.760
he called the pineal gland, but we could

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:20.160
replace that with any kind of

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:21.680
interactive

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:23.240
mechanism.

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:26.040
What do you think about dualism?

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:27.960
>> Well, I think that interactive dualism

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:31.000
might have looked

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:33.160
uh plausible in the 17th century,

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:37.080
but the, you know, the

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:38.320
rise of modern physical science has just

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:41.360
made it look

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:42.600
very implausible that there's any

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:43.320
non

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:44.919
uh

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:49.120
materials

00:05:44.919 --> 00:05:51.680
uh uh, entity that affects us.

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:53.160
So, we really have very strong

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:56.240
confidence,

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:58.480
um, uh, from the success of these

00:05:56.240 --> 00:06:00.120
sciences that we can explain everything

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:01.200
that goes on in the human body and

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:02.840
brain,

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:05.840
uh, uh,

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:08.120
uh, using these sciences. And we we we

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:11.680
don't have any need to postulate some

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:13.840
kind of causal influence from, uh,

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:15.040
some kind of non-material thing.

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:17.360
>> But what But what about I mean

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:20.080
Descartes' original argument for Julian.

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:21.960
So, if I invite people to imagine, close

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:23.800
their eyes, and to imagine a goblin

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:25.440
who's green, holding a sword, and an

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:28.600
orange shield,

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:30.080
that goblin is intangible, is

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:33.400
indivisible,

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:34.880
and presumably is not physical. So, how

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:36.280
can you explain the presence of the

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:38.840
goblin

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:42.440
under a physicalist model?

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:44.200
>> Yeah, so that's an intentional object.

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:45.560
You can think of things that don't

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:47.000
exist.

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:50.360
It doesn't mean that the things you're

00:06:47.000 --> 00:06:52.720
thinking of do exist. So, I don't find

00:06:50.360 --> 00:06:55.080
this a problem. Uh, we have a mental

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:57.800
state which includes maybe having a

00:06:55.080 --> 00:07:00.000
mental image of a goblin. It doesn't

00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:02.880
mean that they're in any sense are

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:05.160
goblins. So, I don't feel that this is a

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:07.880
real challenge to, uh,

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:10.240
the physicalistic point of view. It just

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:12.640
shows that there are mental states that

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:14.440
have intentional objects, and maybe we

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:16.720
have an imagination,

00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:19.360
but I think all that imagination can be

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:20.520
understood on the basis of the physical

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:23.720
sciences.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:25.360
>> So, do you think the word exist

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:26.919
necessarily entails a physical

